Z||000009||Standard||02-17-2001||03:04 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 007DOUG:<BR><B>And best of all, the officer never even showed-up for the trial. COME ON!!!!! <BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If the officer never showed up, the case should have automatically been ruled in your favor and dismissed.  At least, that's the way it works around here.<P><P>------------------<BR>Everybody wants a normal life and a cool car.. <B>Most people settle for the car</B><P>I'm not dumb.  I just have a command of thoroughly useless information<BR>        <BR>AOL: LastMinuteBastrd<BR>AIM: SavedByJebus    -- you'll understand if you watch The Simpsons :D||||reg||1
Z||000010||ponya||02-17-2001||09:34 AM||||If you were to fight the ticket using a Lawyer, about the only good the Lawyer could do would be to ask the Judge to withhold adjudication. That means that no points would be assessed to you license and therefore no<BR>additional premium added to your insurance. As far as the radar unit,I never leave the PD without it being calibrated, so that's a hard one to beat in Court. Hope that helps,  Bob||||reg||1
Z||000011||Oreif||02-17-2001||10:07 AM||||Police radar is a doppler radar system. It has more faults than most people think. Radar Cross Section of the vehicle plays a large portion. On average the radar units (even if calibrated at the start of every day) Can be up to 20% off, on average they are about 2-5% with one vehicle in the radar's range (not really a valid point if your doing 20 over the speed limit)and a lot higher with more vehicles of varing RCS.<BR>The older the radar unit the worse it is for accuracy. The newer ones use a narrower beam so RCS is less of a factor but they still have problems. Laser is a lot more accurate since the officer can pick the exact car to check. But laser can be dangerous to the drivers eye's in dusk/dawn or night time. <P>As for the ticket you got, Unless you want to spend a lot more money getting a lawyer and traveling back to court to fight it, you might as well pay it. The more you try to fight an out-of-state ticket the more expensive it will get. I know from experience that they could add on other B.S. to increase the cost.||||reg||1
Z||000012||bHooper||02-17-2001||10:30 AM||||i had 6 speeding tickets between 1986 and 1994.  i never once payed for points on my insurance.  twice i used an attorney, two times were out of state, and twice i just went to the DA in my college town (he was my neighbor and used to come to my parties).<P>you may also want to check, it may be possible that all you have to do is pay the fine, without any penalty of points on your ins/license<P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000013||007DOUG||02-17-2001||11:08 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Standard:<BR><B> If the officer never showed up, the case should have automatically been ruled in your favor and dismissed.  At least, that's the way it works around here.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Thats what I thought too. But according to my lawyer, that works on bench trials and above. Not the 3-ring circus I went to. Heck, the mayor isn't even an sworn in judge. he's just a normal guy like you and me. BUT, for more $$$ and time, I could have went to the next higher court and had a "real" trial. <P>Send your letter with the payment.<P>I'm lucky like hoop. Somehow, someway, my insurance never increased. Even when I maxed-out my license (18pts.) and lost them for 30 days. Whew!!!<P>||||reg||1
